            <ArticleTitle>Recurrent right iliac fossa pain in patients with previous appendicectomy.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2014-207971</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2014207971</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recurrent appendicitis can occur up to 40 years after appendicectomy. A history of appendicectomy has often led to late diagnosis, as sepsis is attributed to other organs, usually the urinary tract. A case of a patient presenting with retained faecolith and recurrent/stump appendicitis 2 years after laparoscopic appendicectomy is presented. The case for having a low threshold for early CT scanning in patients post-appendicectomy presenting with sepsis to prevent delay in diagnosis is made, and this case is a useful reminder for surgeons to dissect as far as possible to the appendix base. The literature including important medicolegal cases is reviewed.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2015 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
